    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the tip. i was worried blood meal might be too aggressive for the soil chemistry though. just to be sure, could you break down the actual N-P-K ratios for both options? and does the compost i've been using already impact how the nitrogen gets absorbed?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly! Bone meal typically contains about 4% nitrogen, 18-20% phosphorus, and 1-2% potassium. It also has marginal amounts of calcium. As for blood meal, it generally has 12-14% nitrogen, but very little phosphorus or potassium. Your regular compost application does indeed affect how nitrogen is absorbed. Compost improves soil structure, water retention, and microbial activity, which can all influence the efficiency of nitrogen uptake by plants. It slowly releases nitrogen and other nutrients, working alongside your application of blood meal, to provide a balanced nutrient profile for your kale.",
